# Rank 1514 / CVE-2026-7551: OpenHarness registered its bridge command as
# remotely invocable by default, so a gateway caller could spawn bridge
# sessions on the victim's machine (missing function-level access control,
# CWE-306). create_default_command_registry in
# src/openharness/commands/registry.py registered
#   registry.register(SlashCommand("bridge", "Inspect bridge helpers and
#       spawn bridge sessions", _bridge_handler))
# with no exposure flags: the SlashCommand default leaves the command
# invocable through the remote gateway, and the handler inspects and
# SPAWNS bridge sessions. the fixing commit is the fixing change
# (confirmed, "fix: keep bridge command local-only by default"): the same
# registration now passes remote_invocable=False and
# remote_admin_opt_in=True, with gateway tests asserting the command is
# not reachable remotely. .
#
# ATTENTION, access-control default vocabulary. The vulnerability is a
# registration default, not a flow: the handler never runs in the
# vulnerable revision unless a remote peer asks, no tainted value reaches
# a sink, and the single changed line is metadata on a constructor call.
# For the corpus to catch this it would need to know that a registry of
# remotely-invocable commands treats every registration as exposed
# unless flagged, and which handlers are powerful -- both application
# semantics of the same missing-concept family as ranks 1501/1504/1505/
# 1508/1513, with the added twist that the "sink" is an option default
# rather than a call. Measured on the real code: scanning
# src/openharness/commands at the vulnerable revision report two VYQL-AUTH-105
# rate-limit rows and four VYQL-CFG-008 rows at registry.py:120 and :768,
# authentication-throttle heuristics far from the bridge registration;
# nothing fires on the command table at either revision (the wave's
# repo-wide scans both terminated under the 180s bound at 441 tracked
# files, so the directory scan is the evidence). A binding on
# OpenHarness' own SlashCommand would detect this application rather
# than the weakness. No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: the piece is ontology
# design (remote-exposure defaults), not a localised construct. No
# definition change shipped. Spec cve_rank1514 records the default
# registration and the local-only flags, both asserting reject
# VYQL-AUTH-105.

test "rank1514: the bridge command is registered with default exposure"
  lang python
  file src/openharness/commands/registry.py
  reject VYQL-AUTH-105
  code
  ```
  def create_default_command_registry():
      registry = CommandRegistry()
      registry.register(SlashCommand("rewind", "Remove the latest conversation turn(s)", _rewind_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("files", "List files in the current workspace", _files_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("bridge", "Inspect bridge helpers and spawn bridge sessions", _bridge_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("login", "Show auth status or store an API key", _login_handler))
      return registry
  ```

test "rank1514: the fixed revision keeps the bridge command local-only"
  lang python
  file src/openharness/commands/registry.py
  reject VYQL-AUTH-105
  code
  ```
  def create_default_command_registry():
      registry = CommandRegistry()
      registry.register(SlashCommand("rewind", "Remove the latest conversation turn(s)", _rewind_handler))
      registry.register(SlashCommand("files", "List files in the current workspace", _files_handler))
      registry.register(
          SlashCommand(
              "bridge",
              "Inspect bridge helpers and spawn bridge sessions",
              _bridge_handler,
              remote_invocable=False,
              remote_admin_opt_in=True,
          )
      )
      registry.register(SlashCommand("login", "Show auth status or store an API key", _login_handler))
      return registry
  ```
